WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.742
ACID is a common way to describe important transaction properties.

00:00:03.742 --> 00:00:07.957
Atomicity means a related group of changes is treated as one unit.

00:00:07.957 --> 00:00:10.557
Either the intended transaction succeeds or its work can be undone.

00:00:10.557 --> 00:00:14.312
Consistency means database rules and constraints should still hold when a valid transaction finishes.

00:00:14.312 --> 00:00:21.362
Isolation deals with concurrent work so transactions do not incorrectly interfere with each other's

00:00:21.362 --> 00:00:25.692
intermediate states. Durability means that once a transaction is committed in a durable database system,

00:00:25.692 --> 00:00:28.502
the committed result is intended to persist even after failures.

00:00:28.502 --> 00:00:32.922
For this beginner module, the practical takeaway is not to memorize definitions word for word.

00:00:32.922 --> 00:00:39.842
Understand why transactions are useful: they give us a controlled boundary around related changes and a clear

00:00:39.842 --> 00:00:42.497
choice between keeping them and undoing them.

00:00:42.497 --> 00:00:45.552
Transactions let several statements behave as one logical unit.

00:00:45.552 --> 00:00:48.552
That matters when partial success would leave the database inconsistent—for example,

00:00:48.552 --> 00:00:52.592
creating an order header without its order lines or moving money out of one account without recording the

00:00:52.592 --> 00:00:57.600
corresponding destination. COMMIT confirms the unit; ROLLBACK abandons it.
